// Cartesian products up to the full row budget.
//
// A join with no shared variable is a cartesian, and its output row is bounded
// by ROW_MAX like any other plain row. `Wide` is ROW_MAX columns built from a
// (ROW_MAX - 1)-column relation and a 1-column one; `Dup` is the other edge of
// the same budget — a narrow product whose head repeats body variables until it
// reaches ROW_MAX, which is why the target arity cannot be bounded by the
// combined input arity.
//
// Both used to plan thin and then panic every worker with "codegen_cartesian
// unimplemented" while the dataflow was being assembled.

.in
.decl Six(a: number, b: number, c: number, d: number, e: number, f: number)
.input Six.csv
.decl One(g: number)
.input One.csv
.decl Two(p: number, q: number)
.input Two.csv

.printsize
.decl Wide(a: number, b: number, c: number, d: number, e: number, f: number, g: number)
.decl Dup(a: number, b: number, c: number, d: number, e: number, f: number, g: number)

.rule
Wide(A, B, C, D, E, F, G) :- Six(A, B, C, D, E, F), One(G).
Dup(P, Q, G, P, Q, G, P) :- Two(P, Q), One(G).
